  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday Night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1140 PM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nAbove-average high temperatures and predominantly dry conditions\nwill remain the primary story through Thursday beneath broad \nsouthwesterly flow aloft. Highs on Thursday will generally top \nout in the lower to middle 80s, running about 15 degrees above \nseasonal normals. \n\nRain chances will begin to increase from west to east late \nThursday night into Friday as an initial shortwave disturbance \nlifts northeast out of west Texas. Multi-model guidance, along \nwith global ensemble means, and the NBM depict modest moisture \nreturn ahead of this shortwave. Scattered showers are expected to \nspread eastward, initially across east-central Oklahoma during the\ndaytime Friday, before spreading into far eastern Oklahoma and \nnorthwest Arkansas by Friday evening/night. The NBM continues to \ncarry the highest chances of PoPs (40-70%) Saturday morning and \ninto the early afternoon, before significantly decreasing by \nSaturday evening. Severe weather potential remains unlikely, given\nvery marginal instability. In fact, thunder will be hard to come \nby through at least the upcoming weekend. Rainfall amounts should \nmainly stay below one inch through the weekend. \n\nA brief lull in precipitation coverage is anticipated Sunday into\nSunday night as the lead wave departs into the Mid-Mississippi \nValley and shortwave ridging moves across the area. By Sunday \nnight and into Monday morning, a potentially stronger, more \namplified synoptic trough/cut-off low pushes into the central \nCONUS. While deterministic runs and other guidance continue to \nshow considerable run-to-run spread regarding the exact timing, \ndepth, and trajectory of this secondary upper-level disturbance, \nconfidence is increasing in renewed, more widespread shower and \nthunderstorm chances ahead of an associated surface cold front \nearly next week (Monday into Tuesday timeframe). Given high \nmodel/ensemble variance, have stayed close to NBM baseline PoPs \n(40-70%) for Monday into Tuesday. A transition to cooler, more \nseasonable temperatures is expected behind the front toward the \nend of the forecast period.\n\nMejia",
